{"name":"Practical Beige","hex":"#c9b29c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"practical","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Relating to, or based on, practice or action rather than theory or hypothesis.","Being likely to be effective and applicable to a real situation; able to be put to use."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A part of an exam or series of exams in which the candidate has to demonstrate practical ability.","A prop that has some degree of functionality, rather than being a mere imitation."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/practical"},{"term":"beige","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour, variously defined from a pale brown, to a yellow greyish off-white.","Debeige; a kind of woollen or mixed dress goods."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a slightly yellowish gray colour, as that of unbleached wool.","Comfortably dull and unadventurous, in a way that suggests middle-class suburbia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beige"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pragmatism","blocks":[{"text":"Pragmatism is a philosophical tradition that views language and thought as tools for prediction, problem solving, and action, rather than describing, representing, or mirroring reality. Pragmatists contend that most philosophical topics—such as the nature of knowledge, language, concepts, meaning, belief, and science—are best viewed in terms of their practical uses and successes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Charles_Sanders_Peirce_theb3558.jpg/330px-Charles_Sanders_Peirce_theb3558.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pragmatism"},{"title":"Beige","blocks":[{"text":"Beige is variously described as a pale sandy fawn color, a grayish tan, a light-grayish yellowish brown, or a pale to grayish yellow. It takes its name from French, where the word originally meant natural wool that has been neither bleached nor dyed, hence also the color of natural wool."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Saint_Anthony_church_in_Chamboret_07.jpg/330px-Saint_Anthony_church_in_Chamboret_07.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beige"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Hilary_Putnam.jpg/500px-Hilary_Putnam.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Hilary_Putnam.jpg/1280px-Hilary_Putnam.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Hilary_Putnam.jpg/500px-Hilary_Putnam.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hilary Putnam said that the combination of antiskepticism and fallibilism is a central feature of pragmatism.[24][25][26]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHilary_Putnam.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/5f/Chicago_Club_1896.jpg/500px-Chicago_Club_1896.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/5f/Chicago_Club_1896.jpg/500px-Chicago_Club_1896.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The \"Chicago Club\" including Mead, Dewey, Angell, and Moore. Pragmatism is sometimes called American pragmatism because so many of its proponents were and are Americans.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChicago_Club_1896.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/8e/William_james_small.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/8e/William_james_small.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm